\begin{abstract}
The transreal line is the four-part carrier $\T = \R \sqcup \{+\infty\} \sqcup \{-\infty\} \sqcup \{\Phi\}$, on which division is total: $1/0 = +\infty$, $(-1)/0 = -\infty$ and $0/0 = \Phi$ (\emph{nullity}). Analytic statements about $\T$ --- in particular sharpness statements asserting that certain guarded operations cannot be made continuous --- are usually proved for a topology introduced by decree, namely the extended real line $\Rbar$ together with $\Phi$ as an isolated point. We ask whether that choice is forced. We call a compact Hausdorff topology on $\T$ in which the finite fragment is an open copy of $\R$ and $\{\Phi\}$ is open a \emph{transreal compactification}, and prove: (i) these axioms do \emph{not} determine the topology, as witnessed by a circle model in which the line is compactified by the single point named $-\infty$ while $+\infty$ and $\Phi$ are isolated; (ii) adding the requirement that no exceptional point other than $\Phi$ be isolated still does not determine it, as witnessed by the flip model obtained by exchanging the names of the two infinities; (iii) a topology on $\T$ equals the natural one if and only if it is a transreal compactification whose infinities are \emph{oriented ends}; and (iv) every transreal compactification with no isolated infinity is the natural topology or its flip. The engine is an ends argument for $\R$: a compact core produced by Hausdorff separation, connectedness of the two complementary rays, forced tail-ray neighbourhood bases at the infinities, and the compact-to-Hausdorff comparison of topologies. As a consequence, sharpness statements about the division boundary split into two grades: the discontinuity of $x \mapsto x/x$ is \emph{topology-canonical}, holding in every $T_1$ model, while the non-existence of a continuous repair of $y \mapsto 1/y$ at the origin is \emph{ends-canonical}, holding exactly in the models with two honest ends and failing in the circle model, where the merged infinity is the unique continuous repair.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} transreal arithmetic; end compactification; extended real line; two-ended space; division by zero; uniqueness of compactifications; nullity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Total arithmetic and its topological cost}

Ordinary arithmetic on $\R$ is partial: division by zero has no value. \emph{Transreal arithmetic} removes the partiality by enlarging the carrier with three symbols and legislating values for the missing cases. The carrier has four constructors,
\[
\T \;::=\; \fin(x)\ (x \in \R) \;\mid\; +\infty \;\mid\; -\infty \;\mid\; \Phi ,
\]
with $\Phi$ (\emph{nullity}) absorbing the genuinely indeterminate results, so that $\fin(1)/\fin(0) = +\infty$, $\fin(-1)/\fin(0) = -\infty$ and $\fin(0)/\fin(0) = \Phi$.

Totality is cheap; \emph{continuity} is not. A total arithmetic is analytically useful only if its operations respect limits, and to speak of limits one needs a topology on $\T$. The customary choice is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nat}
\T \;\cong\; \Rbar \;\sqcup\; \{\Phi\},
\end{equation}
where $\Rbar = [-\infty, +\infty]$ carries its order topology and $\Phi$ is a separate isolated point. We call \eqref{eq:nat} the \emph{natural topology}, written $\taunat$.

\subsection{The problem and the results}

Every theorem of the form ``operation $F$ cannot be made continuous on $\T$'' is, strictly, a theorem about $\taunat$. If some other reasonable topology existed, such theorems would be artefacts of a modelling decision. We therefore isolate the structural requirements one would insist on and ask whether they characterise \eqref{eq:nat}. They do not as stated (Theorem~\ref{thm:circle}); they do not even after a density requirement (Theorem~\ref{thm:flip}); they do after an \emph{orientation} of the two infinities (Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}); and replacing orientation by mere non-isolation yields a complete classification with exactly two members (Theorem~\ref{thm:class}).

Section~\ref{sec:axioms} fixes the axioms, Section~\ref{sec:nat} the properties of $\taunat$, Section~\ref{sec:ends} the ends argument, Section~\ref{sec:unique} the uniqueness theorem, Section~\ref{sec:sharp} the two counterexample models, Section~\ref{sec:class} the classification, and Section~\ref{sec:division} the consequences for the division boundary. Sections~\ref{sec:algo}--\ref{sec:future} discuss computational content, applications and future work.

\section{The carrier and the axioms}\label{sec:axioms}

Throughout, $\fin : \R \to \T$ is the injection of the finite fragment; $\fin(S) = \{\fin(x) : x \in S\}$ for $S \subseteq \R$; and $I_{>b} = (b,\infty)$, $I_{<b} = (-\infty,b)$. The \emph{exceptional points} are $+\infty$, $-\infty$ and $\Phi$; the first two are the \emph{infinities}. The four constructors are pairwise distinct and no exceptional point lies in the range of $\fin$.

\begin{definition}[Transreal compactification]\label{def:comp}
A topology $\tau$ on $\T$ is a \emph{transreal compactification} if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textbf{(C)}] $(\T,\tau)$ is compact;
\item[\textbf{(H)}] $(\T,\tau)$ is Hausdorff;
\item[\textbf{(L)}] $\fin : \R \to (\T,\tau)$ is an open embedding;
\item[\textbf{(N)}] $\{\Phi\}$ is $\tau$-open.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Orientation]\label{def:orient}
A transreal compactification $\tau$ is \emph{oriented} if
\[
+\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Igt) \qquad\text{and}\qquad -\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Ilt).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[End property]\label{def:end}
A point $a \in \T \setminus \fin(\R)$ is an \emph{end} for $\tau$ if for every $\tau$-open $W \ni a$ and every $c \in \R$ there is $x \in \R$ with $|x| > c$ and $\fin(x) \in W$.
\end{definition}

\section{The natural topology}\label{sec:nat}

Let $\iota : \T \to \Rbar \oplus \{\ast\}$ be the bijection of \eqref{eq:nat}, with $\fin(x) \mapsto \mathrm{inl}(x)$, $\pm\infty \mapsto \mathrm{inl}(\pm\infty)$ and $\Phi \mapsto \mathrm{inr}(\ast)$, and let $\taunat$ be the topology it induces.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:natcomp}
$\taunat$ is a transreal compactification, and it is oriented.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compactness and Hausdorffness transfer along $\iota$ from a disjoint union of a compact interval and a point. Axiom (L) holds since $\R \hookrightarrow \Rbar$ and $\mathrm{inl}$ are open embeddings; (N) holds since $\mathrm{inr}(\ast)$ is clopen. For orientation, $\fin(x) \to +\infty$ as $x \to +\infty$ and eventually $x > 0$, so $+\infty \in \cl \fin(\Igt)$; symmetrically at $-\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Tail-ray bases]\label{prop:tailnat}
If $s$ is $\taunat$-open with $+\infty \in s$, there is $b \in \R$ with $\{+\infty\} \cup \fin(I_{>b}) \subseteq s$; symmetrically, if $-\infty \in s$ there is $b$ with $\{-\infty\} \cup \fin(I_{<b}) \subseteq s$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport $s$ through $\iota$ and restrict to the $\Rbar$ summand: the preimage is an open neighbourhood of $\top$ in $\Rbar$, whose neighbourhood filter is generated by the rays $(b,\top]$. Pull the ray back using injectivity of $\iota$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:notisolated}
Neither $\{+\infty\}$ nor $\{-\infty\}$ is $\taunat$-open.
\end{corollary}

\section{The ends argument}\label{sec:ends}

Fix a transreal compactification $\tau$; all topological notions below refer to $\tau$.

\begin{lemma}[The positive end absorbs the ray]\label{lem:posend}
Assume $+\infty \in \cl \fin(\Igt)$. Then for every open $W \ni +\infty$ and every $c \in \R$ there is $x > c$ with $\fin(x) \in W$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Put $d = |c| \ge 0$. The set $\fin([-d,d])$ is a continuous image of a compact set, hence compact, hence closed by (H). So $W' = W \setminus \fin([-d,d])$ is open and contains $+\infty$, because $+\infty \notin \fin(\R)$. By the closure hypothesis $W'$ meets $\fin(\Igt)$: there is $x > 0$ with $\fin(x) \in W'$. Then $x \notin [-d,d]$ and $x>0$, so $x > d \ge c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The negative end]\label{lem:negend}
Assume $-\infty \in \cl \fin(\Ilt)$. Then for every open $W \ni -\infty$ and every $c \in \R$ there is $x < c$ with $\fin(x) \in W$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Mirror image of Lemma~\ref{lem:posend}: the same compact interval is deleted, and the witness $x<0$ must satisfy $x < -d \le c$ where $d=|c|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unoriented ray separation]\label{lem:rays}
Suppose both infinities are ends in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:end}. Then there exist $M \in \R$ and disjoint open sets $U \ni +\infty$, $V \ni -\infty$ with $\Phi \notin U \cup V$, such that either
\[
\fin(I_{>M}) \subseteq U \text{ and } \fin(I_{<-M}) \subseteq V,
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\fin(I_{>M}) \subseteq V \text{ and } \fin(I_{<-M}) \subseteq U .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By (H) choose disjoint open $U_0 \ni +\infty$, $V_0 \ni -\infty$. Using (N) and the fact that $\{\Phi\}$ is also closed, replace them by the open sets $U_1 = U_0 \cup \{\Phi\}$ and $V_1 = V_0 \setminus \{\Phi\}$, still disjoint. The uncovered set $K = \T \setminus (U_1 \cup V_1)$ is closed in a compact space, hence compact, and omits all exceptional points, so $K \subseteq \fin(\R)$ and $\fin^{-1}(K)$ is a compact, hence bounded, subset of $\R$; choose $M \ge 0$ with $\fin^{-1}(K) \subseteq [-M,M]$. Then the two rays are covered by $U_1 \cup V_1$; each ray is connected, so its image lies wholly in $U_1$ or wholly in $V_1$. If both lay in $U_1$, the neighbourhood $V_1$ of $-\infty$ would contain no $\fin(x)$ with $|x|>M$, contradicting the end property of $-\infty$. Finally put $U = U_1 \setminus \{\Phi\}$ and $V = V_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Oriented ray separation]\label{cor:orays}
If $\tau$ is oriented, the first alternative of Lemma~\ref{lem:rays} holds.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Orientation with Lemmas~\ref{lem:posend}--\ref{lem:negend} gives the end property, so Lemma~\ref{lem:rays} applies. In the second alternative Lemma~\ref{lem:posend} supplies $x > M$ with $\fin(x) \in U$, while $\fin(I_{>M}) \subseteq V$ and $U \cap V = \emptyset$ --- a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Tail rays are neighbourhoods]\label{prop:tailforced}
Let $\tau$ be an oriented transreal compactification. For every $b \in \R$ there is a $\tau$-open $W \ni +\infty$ with $W \subseteq \{+\infty\} \cup \fin(I_{>b})$, and a $\tau$-open $W' \ni -\infty$ with $W' \subseteq \{-\infty\} \cup \fin(I_{<b})$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $M,U,V$ from Corollary~\ref{cor:orays} and set $R = \max(M,|b|)$. Since $\fin([-R,R])$ is compact, hence closed, $W = U \setminus \fin([-R,R])$ is open and contains $+\infty$. A finite point $\fin(x) \in W$ has $|x| > R \ge M$; if $x < -R$ then $\fin(x) \in V$, contradicting disjointness, so $x > R \ge b$. The point $-\infty$ is excluded by disjointness and $\Phi$ because $\Phi \notin U$. The claim at $-\infty$ is symmetric with $W' = V \setminus \fin([-R,R])$.
\end{proof}

\section{The uniqueness theorem}\label{sec:unique}

\begin{proposition}[Comparison]\label{prop:comparison}
If $\tau$ is an oriented transreal compactification then $\tau \le \taunat$: every $\taunat$-open set is $\tau$-open.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $s$ be $\taunat$-open and $a \in s$; we find a $\tau$-open $W$ with $a \in W \subseteq s$. If $a = \fin(x)$, then $\fin^{-1}(s)$ is open in $\R$ and (L) makes $\fin(\fin^{-1}(s))$ a $\tau$-open subset of $s$ containing $a$. If $a = \Phi$, take $W = \{\Phi\}$, open by (N). If $a = +\infty$, Proposition~\ref{prop:tailnat} gives $b$ with $\{+\infty\}\cup\fin(I_{>b}) \subseteq s$ and Proposition~\ref{prop:tailforced} gives a $\tau$-open $W \ni +\infty$ inside it; the case $a = -\infty$ is symmetric. Hence $s$ is a $\tau$-neighbourhood of each of its points, so $\tau$-open.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the oriented two-point transreal compactification]\label{thm:unique}
Let $\tau$ be a compact Hausdorff topology on $\T$ for which $\fin$ is an open embedding, $\{\Phi\}$ is open, and
\[
+\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Igt), \qquad -\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Ilt).
\]
Then $\tau = \taunat$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:comparison} gives $\tau \le \taunat$, i.e.\ the identity $(\T,\tau) \to (\T,\taunat)$ is continuous. Conversely, if $s$ is $\tau$-open then $\T\setminus s$ is $\tau$-closed, hence $\tau$-compact by (C), hence its image under the continuous identity is $\taunat$-compact and therefore $\taunat$-closed, since $\taunat$ is Hausdorff. So $s$ is $\taunat$-open, and the two topologies agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation]\label{thm:charac}
For a topology $\tau$ on $\T$: $\tau = \taunat$ if and only if $\tau$ is a transreal compactification with $+\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Igt)$ and $-\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\Ilt)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Forward, Proposition~\ref{prop:natcomp}; backward, Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The last step of Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} is the standard fact that a compact topology finer than a Hausdorff topology equals it. It converts the one-sided information produced by the ends argument into an equality with no further analysis of $\tau$.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpness: two exotic models}\label{sec:sharp}

\subsection{The circle model}

Let $\R^{+} = \R \cup \{\infty_{\mathrm c}\}$ be the one-point compactification of the line. Define the bijection
\[
\gamma(\fin x) = \mathrm{inl}(x), \quad \gamma(-\infty) = \mathrm{inl}(\infty_{\mathrm c}), \quad \gamma(+\infty) = \mathrm{inr}(1), \quad \gamma(\Phi) = \mathrm{inr}(0)
\]
into $\R^{+} \oplus \{0,1\}$, and let $\taucirc$ be the induced topology.

\begin{theorem}[The circle model]\label{thm:circle}
$\taucirc$ is a transreal compactification with $\taucirc \ne \taunat$. Moreover $\{+\infty\}$ is $\taucirc$-open and $+\infty \notin \cl_{\taucirc} \fin(\R)$, while
\[
-\infty \in \cl_{\taucirc}\fin(\Igt) \quad\text{and}\quad -\infty \in \cl_{\taucirc}\fin(\Ilt).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\gamma$ is a bijection, so $\taucirc$ makes it a homeomorphism onto a compact Hausdorff space (the one-point compactification of a locally compact Hausdorff space, plus two discrete points): (C) and (H). (L) holds because $\R \hookrightarrow \R^{+}$ and $\mathrm{inl}$ are open embeddings; (N) and openness of $\{+\infty\}$ hold because the two points of the second summand are clopen. Since $\{+\infty\}$ is not $\taunat$-open (Corollary~\ref{cor:notisolated}), the topologies differ. The closure statements express that $\fin(x) \to -\infty$ as $|x| \to \infty$ in either direction, which is the defining property of $\infty_{\mathrm c}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The flip model}

Let $\sigma : \T \to \T$ exchange $+\infty$ and $-\infty$ and fix everything else; it is an involution. Let $\tauflip$ be the topology induced by $\sigma$ from $\taunat$, so that $s$ is $\tauflip$-open exactly when $\sigma(s)$ is $\taunat$-open.

\begin{theorem}[The flip model]\label{thm:flip}
$\tauflip$ is a transreal compactification, neither $\{+\infty\}$ nor $\{-\infty\}$ is $\tauflip$-open, and $\tauflip \ne \taunat$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sigma : (\T,\tauflip) \to (\T,\taunat)$ is a homeomorphism, so (C), (H) and non-isolation transfer. (L) holds because $\sigma \circ \fin = \fin$, and (N) because $\sigma(\{\Phi\}) = \{\Phi\}$. Transporting $-\infty \in \cl_{\taunat}\fin(\Ilt)$ through $\sigma$ gives $+\infty \in \cl_{\tauflip}\fin(\Ilt)$, whereas $+\infty \notin \cl_{\taunat}\fin(\Ilt)$; hence the topologies differ.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness]\label{cor:sharp}
Neither Definition~\ref{def:comp} alone nor Definition~\ref{def:comp} together with the density requirement that no exceptional point other than $\Phi$ be isolated determines the topology. The orientation hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} is indispensable.
\end{corollary}

\section{Classification}\label{sec:class}

\begin{lemma}[Non-isolated implies limit of finite points]\label{lem:nonisolated}
Let $\tau$ be a transreal compactification. If $\{+\infty\}$ is not $\tau$-open then $+\infty \in \cl_\tau \fin(\R)$; symmetrically for $-\infty$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise some open $W \ni +\infty$ misses $\fin(\R)$, so $W \subseteq \{+\infty,-\infty,\Phi\}$; removing the clopen $\{\Phi\}$ and separating $+\infty$ from $-\infty$ by (H) shrinks $W$ to the open set $\{+\infty\}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Limit of finite points implies end]\label{lem:limend}
Let $\tau$ be a transreal compactification and $a$ an exceptional point with $a \in \cl_\tau \fin(\R)$. Then $a$ is an end in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:end}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
As in Lemma~\ref{lem:posend}, delete the compact-hence-closed set $\fin([-|c|,|c|])$ from $W$ and use $a \notin \fin(\R)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Orientation dichotomy]\label{prop:dichotomy}
Let $\tau$ be a transreal compactification in which neither $\{+\infty\}$ nor $\{-\infty\}$ is open. Then either
\[
+\infty \in \cl_\tau\fin(\Igt) \text{ and } -\infty \in \cl_\tau\fin(\Ilt),
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
+\infty \in \cl_\tau\fin(\Ilt) \text{ and } -\infty \in \cl_\tau\fin(\Igt).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemmas~\ref{lem:nonisolated}--\ref{lem:limend} give both infinities the end property, so Lemma~\ref{lem:rays} yields $M$ and disjoint $U \ni +\infty$, $V \ni -\infty$ distributing the two rays in one of two orders. In the first order, let $W \ni +\infty$ be open and apply the end property to $W \cap U$ with $c = \max(M,0)$: the resulting $x$ with $|x| > c$ cannot be negative, since $x < -M$ would place $\fin(x)$ in $V$. Hence $W$ meets $\fin(\Igt)$, i.e.\ $+\infty \in \cl\fin(\Igt)$. The remaining three assertions are symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Transport along the flip]\label{lem:transport}
For a topology $\tau$ on $\T$ let $\sigma^{*}\tau$ be the topology induced by $\sigma$ from $\tau$. Then $\sigma^{*}(\sigma^{*}\tau) = \tau$, $\sigma^{*}\taunat = \tauflip$, $\sigma^{*}\tau$ is a transreal compactification whenever $\tau$ is, and for all $S \subseteq \R$ and $a \in \T$,
\[
a \in \cl_{\sigma^{*}\tau}\fin(S) \iff \sigma(a) \in \cl_{\tau}\fin(S).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sigma$ is an involutive bijection, so inducing twice returns $\tau$, and $\sigma$ is a homeomorphism $(\T,\sigma^{*}\tau) \to (\T,\tau)$; (C), (H), (N) transfer, and (L) because $\sigma \circ \fin = \fin$. The closure equivalence is homeomorphism-invariance together with $\sigma(\fin(S)) = \fin(S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification]\label{thm:class}
Let $\tau$ be a compact Hausdorff topology on $\T$ for which $\fin$ is an open embedding, $\{\Phi\}$ is open, and neither $\{+\infty\}$ nor $\{-\infty\}$ is open. Then $\tau = \taunat$ or $\tau = \tauflip$, and these are distinct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:dichotomy}, either $\tau$ is oriented, and then $\tau = \taunat$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}; or it is anti-oriented, in which case Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} makes $\sigma^{*}\tau$ an oriented transreal compactification, so $\sigma^{*}\tau = \taunat$ and
\[
\tau = \sigma^{*}(\sigma^{*}\tau) = \sigma^{*}\taunat = \tauflip .
\]
Distinctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:flip}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:oneclause}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:class}, the single additional condition $+\infty \in \cl_\tau\fin(\Igt)$ forces $\tau = \taunat$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The alternative $\tau = \tauflip$ would give $-\infty \in \cl_{\taunat}\fin(\Igt)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}, contradicted by the $\taunat$-open neighbourhood $\{-\infty\} \cup \fin(\Ilt)$ of $-\infty$, which misses $\fin(\Igt)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:class} is the classical statement in disguise: a two-point-remainder compactification of the locally compact, two-ended real line in which both remainder points are limit points is the end compactification, the only residual freedom being which end is labelled $+\infty$.
\end{remark}

\section{Consequences for the division boundary}\label{sec:division}

Write
\[
\rec_v(y) = \begin{cases} v, & y = 0,\\ \fin(1/y), & y \ne 0,\end{cases}
\qquad
\mathrm{sq}(x) = \fin(x)/\fin(x) = \begin{cases} \Phi, & x = 0,\\ \fin(1), & x \ne 0. \end{cases}
\]

\begin{theorem}[Self-division is topology-canonical]\label{thm:selfdiv}
In every $T_1$ topology on $\T$, the map $\mathrm{sq} : \R \to \T$ is discontinuous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\{\fin(1)\}$ is closed in a $T_1$ space, so $\T \setminus \{\fin(1)\}$ is open and contains $\mathrm{sq}(0) = \Phi$; its preimage is $\{0\}$, not a neighbourhood of $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reciprocal repair is ends-canonical]\label{thm:recip}
In $\taunat$, and likewise in $\tauflip$, the map $\rec_v$ is discontinuous at $0$ for every $v \in \T$. Consequently, by Theorem~\ref{thm:class}, in every transreal compactification with no isolated infinity and for every $v$, $\rec_v$ is discontinuous at the origin.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In $\taunat$ the one-sided limits of $\fin(1/y)$ at $0$ are $+\infty$ and $-\infty$; uniqueness of limits in a Hausdorff space excludes any $v$. For $\tauflip$, note $\sigma \circ \rec_v = \rec_{\sigma(v)}$ and that $\sigma$ is an inducing map $(\T,\tauflip) \to (\T,\taunat)$, so continuity of $\rec_v$ at $0$ for $\tauflip$ is equivalent to continuity of $\rec_{\sigma(v)}$ at $0$ for $\taunat$, which fails.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The circle model repairs the reciprocal, uniquely]\label{thm:circrepair}
In $\taucirc$ the map $\rec_{-\infty}$ is continuous on all of $\R$, and if $\rec_v$ is continuous at $0$ for $\taucirc$ then $v = -\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $r : \R \to \R^{+}$ be $r(0) = \infty_{\mathrm c}$ and $r(y) = 1/y$ otherwise. Away from $0$, $r$ is $y \mapsto 1/y$ followed by the open embedding $\R \hookrightarrow \R^{+}$. At $0$: $y \mapsto 1/y$ tends to the cocompact filter along the punctured neighbourhood filter of $0$, because the punctured filter is the supremum of the two one-sided filters, along which $1/y$ exceeds every bound and falls below every bound respectively; and $\mathrm{inl}$ tends to $\infty_{\mathrm c}$ along the cocompact filter. Adding the pure filter at $0$, on which $r$ is constantly $\infty_{\mathrm c}$, gives continuity at $0$. Since $\gamma \circ \rec_{-\infty} = \mathrm{inl} \circ r$ and $\taucirc$ is induced by $\gamma$, $\rec_{-\infty}$ is continuous. Uniqueness: $\rec_v$ and $\rec_{-\infty}$ agree off the origin, so continuity of both at $0$ makes them have the same limit along the punctured filter, and Hausdorffness forces $v = -\infty$.
\end{proof}

