The guard on self-division is forced by arithmetic; the guard on the reciprocal is forced by the geometry of the remainder. A compactification that merges the two ends removes the need for the second guard, at the cost of destroying the order structure of the infinities on which transreal multiplication depends.

\section{Algorithmic content}\label{sec:algo}

Each model is presented by neighbourhood bases at the exceptional points, parametrised by a real threshold:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{natural:}\ & N_b(+\infty) = \{+\infty\}\cup\fin(I_{>b}), && N_b(-\infty) = \{-\infty\}\cup\fin(I_{<-b}), && N(\Phi)=\{\Phi\};\\
\text{flip:}\ & \text{the same two families with labels exchanged}; \\
\text{circle:}\ & N_b(-\infty) = \{-\infty\}\cup\fin(\{|x|>b\}), && N(+\infty)=\{+\infty\}, && N(\Phi)=\{\Phi\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Three algorithms decide the qualitative questions in time linear in the number of probe thresholds: (A) \emph{end classification}, which probes each exceptional point for positive-end, negative-end, merged or isolated behaviour and thereby identifies the model, by Theorems~\ref{thm:unique}, \ref{thm:circle} and \ref{thm:class}; (B) \emph{compact core radius and ray assignment}, the computational shadow of Lemma~\ref{lem:rays}, which exhibits the least $M$ beyond which the two rays are separated and records which separator receives which ray; and (C) the \emph{repairability test}, which computes the two one-sided limits of $\fin(1/y)$ in the model's own neighbourhood structure and reports the unique repairing value when they agree, reproducing Theorems~\ref{thm:recip} and \ref{thm:circrepair}.

\section{Applications}\label{sec:apps}

\emph{Robust numerics.} Systems returning $\pm\infty$ and a nullity-like value instead of raising exceptions inherit this analysis: if the infinite values are to be honest limits of large magnitudes, there is no design freedom beyond the sign convention, and the reciprocal must be guarded at zero. Merging the two infinities into a single unsigned infinity makes the reciprocal continuous but destroys the compatibility of the infinities with order and with multiplication by signs.

\emph{Exact real computation.} The forced tail-ray bases of Proposition~\ref{prop:tailforced} are exactly the observations ``$x$ exceeds $b$'' available to a computation on a stream representation of a real number; the uniqueness theorem says the observational topology of a two-ended total real arithmetic is determined by the observations available at the infinities.

\emph{Compactification design.} The argument is a template: to check that a proposed finite-remainder compactification of a locally compact space is the end compactification, verify that no remainder point is isolated and that each absorbs a distinct complementary component of a compact exhaustion.

\section{Discussion and future work}\label{sec:future}

The conjecture that ``compact Hausdorff, open line, isolated nullity'' forces the natural topology is false as stated (circle model), still false after adding density (flip model), and true after adding an orientation of the two ends (Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}). Theorem~\ref{thm:class} closes the space of models under the density hypothesis: exactly two.

\paragraph{An ends functor for arbitrary locally compact carriers.} The argument used only that $\R$ is locally compact, that the complement of a compact core has exactly two components, and that those components are connected. Replacing ``two rays'' by ``the components of the complements of a compact exhaustion'' should yield a functorial statement: finite-remainder compactifications of a locally compact Hausdorff space with finitely many ends are exactly the quotients of its end compactification.

\paragraph{Arithmetic rigidity.} The flip ambiguity is a purely topological symmetry which transreal arithmetic breaks: $\fin(1)\cdot(+\infty) = +\infty$ while $\fin(1)\cdot(-\infty) = -\infty$, and $\sigma$ does not commute with multiplication by a positive scalar. The orientation that topology cannot supply may therefore be derivable from separate continuity of multiplication; with the classification in hand this reduces to a single computation on the flip model.

\paragraph{Guard necessity as an invariant of the remainder.} The circle model repairs $1/y$ precisely because its remainder is a quotient of the end space. This suggests: an unbounded continuous real function extends continuously to a compactification if and only if the remainder separates its limit behaviour along the ends. Theorems~\ref{thm:recip} and \ref{thm:circrepair} are two verified instances.

\paragraph{Dropping the nullity axiom.} Isolation of $\Phi$ was used only to keep the uncovered compact core inside the line. Dropping it should enlarge the space of models to a lattice in which $\Phi$ may itself be attached to the line; whether that lattice has a largest element, and how the two grades of sharpness behave across it, is open.

